1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,096
The saved Week 16 index lists eleven reading rows. The assigned focus
ranges from learner-specific remediation to procedure review,
provincial examination material, case reasoning, medication review and
measurement flashcards.

2
00:00:15,096 --> 00:00:30,048
Nancy Caroline’s Canadian eighth edition is the learner-confirmed
principal examination text. The selected assessment chapters below are
a separate bridge; they are not a twelfth school row and do not
replace any of the eleven assignments.

3
00:00:30,048 --> 00:00:51,384
The reading panel now contains the reviewed school-folder bodies
rather than short candidate placeholders. Keep three distinctions
visible: a named chapter can be explained even when a school page
number conflicts; a checklist index is not every listed procedure
body; and a current clinical comparison is not proof of the school
edition.

4
00:00:51,384 --> 00:01:19,416
The cardiovascular copy has seventy-nine physical pages, while the
school locator reaches page three hundred nineteen. The complete seven
named chapters are explained, but literal page equivalence is
unresolved. The procedure reader now adds primary Pinel teaching and
distinguishes the actual PR30 resources from the mislabeled
attachment. Source identity differences remain visible rather than
becoming invented instructions.

5
00:01:19,416 --> 00:01:34,200
For a case-focused chapter, build a timeline and separate reported
facts, direct observations, unknowns and assumptions. For a skills
checklist, identify the safety gate, expected result and reassessment
point.

6
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:51,480
Keep source identity beside each note. A candidate nursing or
pharmacology textbook can explain a general concept, but current BC
Emergency Health Services direction, BC legislation, provider scope
and instructor guidance control operational practice.

7
00:01:51,480 --> 00:02:11,088
Start with the assignment directory in Reading mode. The
cardiovascular guide has all seven chapter routes, the pharmacology
guide has twenty-five chapters in six units, and the procedure guide
has code-specific rounds. These are organized components of weekly
teaching, not hundreds of unrelated lessons.

8
00:02:11,088 --> 00:02:29,088
Read both the source notice and the explanation. When the school names
an individual gap, select the matching route from an actual observed
difficulty. Do not manufacture a personal deficiency or mark a missing
school appendix complete because a related nursing index is available.

9
00:02:29,088 --> 00:02:52,680
For the school bradycardia case, connect electrical rate to mechanical
output. Cardiac output depends on both rate and stroke volume, and
tissue perfusion also depends on pressure and vascular conditions. A
low displayed rate alone does not tell you whether output is adequate.
Compare consciousness, skin, symptoms, blood pressure and the measured
trend.

10
00:02:52,680 --> 00:03:15,912
If the learner gap is respiratory, distinguish movement of air, gas
exchange and oxygen delivery. If it is neurological, connect an
observed change to brain function without assigning a lesion from one
sign. The A&P routes support targeted explanation; they do not impose
an invented fixed reading range on the school’s individualized
assignment.

11
00:03:15,912 --> 00:03:41,040
Read the seven chapters as one circulation: electrical activation
drives contraction; filling and resistance affect flow; valve or
congenital abnormalities alter pressure or direction; and coronary
supply must meet demand. Heart failure can occur with preserved
ejection fraction, so a preserved fraction does not prove normal
filling pressures or absence of congestion.

12
00:03:41,040 --> 00:04:06,408
In a breathless patient, contrast pump failure with an arrhythmia, an
ischaemic process and a non-cardiac cause instead of forcing every cue
into one diagnosis. New chest symptoms, a changing ECG or a concerning
perfusion trend require the authorized assessment and escalation
pathway. A summary table is not a rule that excludes disease when one
familiar sign is absent.

13
00:04:06,408 --> 00:04:27,480
Start the six-unit review with the difference between what the body
does to a medicine and what a medicine does to the body. Poor intake,
renal or hepatic impairment, timing and formulation can change
exposure. A medicine list without the last actual dose or a recent
change may conceal the most important part of the history.

14
00:04:27,480 --> 00:04:53,376
Use the early chapters for safe administration, legal and ethical
reasoning, calculations, teaching, supplements and complementary
products. An OTC label or natural origin does not remove interaction
or toxicity risk. Dimensional analysis checks units, but the result
still needs the correct patient, authorized order, concentration,
route and a plausibility check.

15
00:04:53,376 --> 00:05:15,912
The immunity, anti-infective and cancer chapters connect treatment
targets with host risk. Distinguish immune suppression from immune
stimulation and an allergic reaction from an unrelated adverse effect.
An antibiotic does not treat every cause of fever, and changing
symptoms can reflect a complication rather than failure to use a
stronger drug.

16
00:05:15,912 --> 00:05:41,664
In a recently treated cancer patient who becomes unwell, obtain the
treatment and symptom timeline and assess immediate threats without
assuming the problem is anxiety or an expected side effect. Report
fever, deterioration and relevant treatment information through the
actual pathway. This review explains risk recognition, not an
independent antimicrobial or oncology prescription.

17
00:05:41,664 --> 00:06:07,104
Use the neural foundation before memorizing drug names. Receptor
location and action explain why one product can change pulse,
pressure, secretions, alertness or breathing. The same symptom can
arise from illness, a prescribed dose, accumulation, a missed
treatment or another exposure, so reconstruct the timeline before
deciding which mechanism best fits.

18
00:06:07,104 --> 00:06:34,200
The neurological, psychiatric and substance-use routes preserve
important distinctions: withdrawal can occur despite a positive
alcohol level; ongoing opioid agonist treatment is not simply a short
weaning course; and apparent improvement after a reversal medicine may
not last as long as the original exposure. Continue the required
monitoring and communication rather than treating one response as a
final diagnosis.

19
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:58,896
Link cardiovascular drug purpose to the patient’s findings. A medicine
that lowers pressure or slows the node may have a useful chronic role
yet be relevant to today’s poor perfusion. Diuretics can change volume
and electrolytes; treatment effect and adverse effect must be assessed
together. Never infer that all slow rhythms or all rapid rhythms share
one medication response.

20
00:06:58,896 --> 00:07:27,336
Antiplatelets, anticoagulants and thrombolytics act at different parts
of haemostasis and have different indications, monitoring and reversal
limits. A normal INR does not exclude clinically relevant direct oral
anticoagulant exposure. Lipid treatments primarily address longer-term
risk; symptom absence does not prove treatment is unnecessary. Use
chapter contrasts before recalling a product from its name ending.

21
00:07:27,336 --> 00:07:52,848
The named sixth unit includes all three respiratory chapters, even
though the school’s literal page endpoint stops earlier. Distinguish
upper-airway symptom products, lower-airway bronchodilators and anti-
inflammatory controllers. A brief improvement in wheeze does not prove
all risk is resolved. An acceptable saturation does not prove that the
patient is ventilating adequately.

22
00:07:52,848 --> 00:08:17,232
Check the actual formulation and delivery device before reasoning
about benefit or harm. Children are not small adults for cough-and-
cold products; combination ingredients and sedating effects matter. If
a patient is deteriorating, reassess airway, breathing, mental status
and authorized escalation needs instead of stacking OTC products or
treating the monitor number alone.

23
00:08:17,232 --> 00:08:38,688
The code-specific reading rounds explain the available procedure
bodies and identify advanced-provider material. For the two-hundred-
twenty-kilogram movement case, capacity, patient condition, route and
the whole equipment system must be assessed before movement. A
patient’s ability to stand or an isolated device label cannot replace
that plan.

24
00:08:38,688 --> 00:09:05,496
For Pinel, use the actual manufacturer instructions together with the
current service policies, not a title in an old contents page. For
transfusion, explain the supplied checklist and pediatric syringe
method within their actual roles. A volumetric label on a syringe
manual does not change the device. Do not invent a missing method or
turn a hospital example into new independent PCP authority.

25
00:09:05,496 --> 00:09:30,864
The CPSC guide groups the complete Appendix Two index and explains the
assigned glucose-assessment pages. A checklist entry tells you that a
procedure exists; it does not supply every instruction or make an
unfamiliar ward skill appropriate for a PCP. Check indication,
equipment, patient explanation, infection-control boundaries, response
and the actual authorized procedure.

26
00:09:30,864 --> 00:09:54,720
The CNS guide teaches the identified cardiac chapter and clinical-
judgment content. Its missing Appendix Two pointer remains separate
from the available CPSC index. Use cardiac assessment to connect
history and physical findings, but do not let a checklist delay
immediate support or convert an educational nursing assessment into a
paramedic treatment order.

27
00:09:54,720 --> 00:10:19,296
The Chapter Seven flashcards are technique retrieval, not just number
memorization. Explain temperature method, pulse and respirations,
pressure technique and saturation limitations. In the large-patient
case, choose a cuff using the measured arm and manufacturer range;
body weight does not determine cuff size. A poor fit can distort the
pressure you use to judge perfusion.

28
00:10:19,296 --> 00:10:41,616
When the measurement conflicts with appearance, check the method and
signal, repeat appropriately and continue immediate care. Document why
a reading is uncertain rather than silently discarding it or reporting
false precision. The twelve source cards are reviewed in the guide;
supervised practice still establishes hand position, fit and
measurement quality.

29
00:10:41,616 --> 00:11:04,752
In the unfolding case, do not leak later information into an earlier
decision. At each point state what is known now, its source, the
important unknown and the priority it supports. A patient can improve
in one domain while a functional, pain or safety problem remains.
Reassessing only the number that first worried you can miss the next
threat.

30
00:11:04,752 --> 00:11:25,848
Use the FON clinical-judgment chain to explain why a new cue changes
your plan. A changed plan is not proof that the first clinician was
careless; it may reflect new information. Separate observation from
interpretation, choose an action within current authority, then
explain the actual response and the next decision.

31
00:11:25,848 --> 00:11:48,432
The MSN outcome-evaluation reading asks you to compare a planned
result with evidence, not to count completed tasks. State the intended
observable change, the actual finding, time and method. If breathing
appears easier but consciousness falls, report both. You cannot call
the overall goal met by selecting only the favourable result.

32
00:11:48,432 --> 00:12:08,328
If a goal is not met, reassess the patient, original assumption,
intervention delivery and measurement quality. Do not automatically
repeat or intensify treatment. Communicate the expected-versus-actual
response and unresolved concern so the next clinician can act on the
same evidence.

33
00:12:08,328 --> 00:12:33,360
The mental-health case dissection separates what the person said, what
you observed and what you infer. In an anxious or distressed patient,
use calm neutral language, one question at a time and enough space to
answer. Do not diagnose from a short fragment or allow a psychiatric
history to explain away abnormal breathing, perfusion, glucose or
consciousness.

34
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:58,776
Reassess safety and the response to communication as the case
develops. Explain privacy without promising absolute secrecy when
safety requires appropriate sharing. During debrief, identify the
specific new fact that changed concern, the support you sought and
what still needs follow-up. These are observable clinical behaviours
rather than labels such as difficult or non-compliant.

35
00:12:58,776 --> 00:13:24,144
The BC Provincial Examination Guidelines reader distinguishes the
school’s saved version, the literal pages and the grading material
reached by the named topic. It also identifies the later comparison
rather than mixing editions. Provincial exam deficiencies and result-
review procedures belong to that licensing system; they are not
automatically AET Mock OSCE pass marks or critical-fail rules.

36
00:13:24,144 --> 00:13:48,000
Use feedback by recording the observed action, sequence, consequence
and actual criterion, then choose one corrected behaviour for the next
drill. The available school plan requires extensive documentation and
formal handover. It does not supply a numerical station rubric, so
neither an original practice answer nor a polished handover can
certify a school pass.

37
00:13:48,000 --> 00:14:04,056
For each mock case, name one idea from the reading that changed your
assessment, sequencing, measurement, documentation or handover. If no
decision changed, explain what knowledge gap remains rather than
forcing a textbook connection.

38
00:14:04,056 --> 00:14:16,488
A reading guide supports revision; it does not certify clinical
competence. Only supervised school practice and the instructor’s
actual criteria can establish performance in the assigned station.

39
00:14:16,488 --> 00:14:42,000
Close the notes and walk through the school bradycardia and movement
case from arrival to handover. State what you know and do not know.
Explain which reading changes each decision: measurement for reliable
pressure, cardiovascular and pharmacology reasoning for the clinical
picture, procedures for safe movement, and case chapters for response
and communication. Do not invent extra case observations.

40
00:14:42,000 --> 00:15:05,232
When you cannot explain one step, reopen that exact chapter or round,
then rehearse again using an actual observed gap. The aim is one
coherent clinical performance, not recalling a pile of isolated facts.
Reading completion, media playback, practical competence and official
school assessment remain different kinds of evidence.

41
00:15:05,232 --> 00:15:28,920
A frightened patient is not an equipment problem. Start with safety,
communication and possible illness, and attempt reasonable de-
escalation. If restraint becomes necessary, establish the applicable
authority and seek appropriate clinical advice. Explain the plan and
the conditions for ending it. A product demonstration cannot make that
decision for you.

42
00:15:28,920 --> 00:15:49,560
Use the least restrictive suitable method for the shortest necessary
time. The reader distinguishes current service policies from the
manufacturer instructions. It also identifies contradictions in those
instructions. In supervised practice, name the actual model and
approved configuration before describing how its parts connect.

43
00:15:49,560 --> 00:16:10,224
Before use, identify the compatible parts and demonstrate both locking
and release. A click alone is not enough. During application, check
fit and the actual attachment point, then assess breathing, skin and
distal circulation. A later recheck never excuses an immediately
unsafe fit.

44
00:16:10,224 --> 00:16:35,160
A waist belt, pelvic component and lateral attachments have different
jobs. An extender changes length, not automatically load capacity. A
manufacturer photograph with hands behind the body does not establish
an approved transport position. Never place or transport a restrained
person face down; follow the actual service policy and maintain
patient access.

45
00:16:35,160 --> 00:16:57,912
In the original case, the patient becomes unusually quiet after
restriction. Stop interpreting compliance as recovery. Reassess the
airway, ventilation, perfusion and possible restraint-related harm,
obtain help and coordinate safe adjustment or release with necessary
support. Continue looking for the underlying medical cause.

46
00:16:57,912 --> 00:17:21,096
Document the observed risk, alternatives, chosen method, response,
monitoring, interventions and clinical communication. Explain what
would permit discontinuation, reassess frequently and reduce
restriction as soon as safe. Your rehearsal records observable
decisions and omissions; it does not certify a skill or create an
official school score.
